1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,561
Sheep.

2
00:00:01,098 --> 00:00:02,109
Meet two sheep.

3
00:00:02,272 --> 00:00:02,908
Same meadow.

4
00:00:02,970 --> 00:00:03,807
Same job.

5
00:00:03,969 --> 00:00:04,793
Same wool.

6
00:00:05,006 --> 00:00:07,190
Thirty years later, one of them is rich.

7
00:00:07,652 --> 00:00:08,950
The other one is Gary.

8
00:00:09,675 --> 00:00:11,086
Gary didn't work less.

9
00:00:11,335 --> 00:00:12,995
Gary actually worked more.

10
00:00:13,619 --> 00:00:14,518
So what happened?

11
00:00:14,568 --> 00:00:16,703
The rich sheep knew one thing that I know.

12
00:00:17,327 --> 00:00:18,688
Today, I'm going to tell you.

13
00:00:18,788 --> 00:00:22,521
And at the end, I'll tell you the true story of a janitor who beat Wall Street.

14
00:00:22,858 --> 00:00:24,168
With a broom.

15
00:00:24,510 --> 00:00:26,283
There are only two ways to get wool.

16
00:00:26,682 --> 00:00:27,394
Way one.

17
00:00:27,855 --> 00:00:29,129
You grow it yourself.

18
00:00:29,466 --> 00:00:30,315
Every day.

19
00:00:31,039 --> 00:00:32,250
With your own body.

20
00:00:32,786 --> 00:00:35,409
Stop working, the wool stops.

21
00:00:35,945 --> 00:00:36,769
Way two.

22
00:00:37,169 --> 00:00:39,315
You own something that grows wool for you.

23
00:00:39,853 --> 00:00:40,627
A shop.

24
00:00:41,088 --> 00:00:42,325
A house you rent out.

25
00:00:42,661 --> 00:00:43,898
A piece of a big company.

26
00:00:43,923 --> 00:00:45,783
These things grow wool while you sleep.

27
00:00:46,182 --> 00:00:47,481
Gary has way one.

28
00:00:47,768 --> 00:00:49,279
Rich sheep have way two.

29
00:00:49,815 --> 00:00:50,539
That's it.

30
00:00:50,701 --> 00:00:51,963
That's the whole secret.

31
00:00:52,150 --> 00:00:54,272
But let me show you why it's so powerful.

32
00:00:54,607 --> 00:00:56,318
First, some things eat wool.

33
00:00:56,479 --> 00:00:57,716
Gary gets a raise.

34
00:00:58,115 --> 00:00:59,963
And Gary buys a brand new car.

35
00:01:00,499 --> 00:01:02,796
The moment he drives it home, it's worth less.

36
00:01:03,083 --> 00:01:04,507
Every year, a little less.

37
00:01:04,968 --> 00:01:05,980
Plus insurance.

38
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:07,041
Plus gas.

39
00:01:07,128 --> 00:01:08,177
Plus repairs.

40
00:01:08,639 --> 00:01:10,099
The car doesn't grow wool.

41
00:01:10,187 --> 00:01:11,036
It eats wool.

42
00:01:11,136 --> 00:01:11,773
New phone.

43
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:12,459
Eats wool.

44
00:01:12,559 --> 00:01:13,408
Bigger apartment.

45
00:01:13,445 --> 00:01:14,194
Eats wool.

46
00:01:14,594 --> 00:01:17,790
Every raise Gary ever got, he fed to something that eats wool.

47
00:01:18,077 --> 00:01:19,799
It's called lifestyle creep.

48
00:01:19,937 --> 00:01:20,761
More wool in.

49
00:01:20,885 --> 00:01:21,760
More wool out.

50
00:01:22,608 --> 00:01:24,456
Gary stays exactly where he was.

51
00:01:24,918 --> 00:01:26,266
Just with a nicer car.

52
00:01:26,603 --> 00:01:28,913
Here's something sheep get wrong all the time.

53
00:01:29,637 --> 00:01:30,649
Who looks rich?

54
00:01:30,836 --> 00:01:33,245
The sheep with the shiny car and the giant phone.

55
00:01:33,582 --> 00:01:35,392
But a lot of that shine is borrowed.

56
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:39,686
In the nineties, two researchers studied thousands of American millionaires.

57
00:01:40,535 --> 00:01:43,431
They wrote a famous book called The Millionaire Next Door.

58
00:01:44,055 --> 00:01:45,229
What did they find?

59
00:01:45,853 --> 00:01:48,337
Many millionaires lived in ordinary houses.

60
00:01:48,737 --> 00:01:50,359
Drove ordinary cars.

61
00:01:50,759 --> 00:01:52,869
And spent much less than they earned.

62
00:01:53,593 --> 00:01:55,853
Rich sheep often look like Gary.

63
00:01:56,852 --> 00:01:58,412
Gary looks like a rich sheep.

64
00:01:58,873 --> 00:02:00,010
On credit.

65
00:02:00,345 --> 00:02:01,919
Now look at the rich sheep.

66
00:02:02,542 --> 00:02:04,041
She gets the same raise.

67
00:02:04,890 --> 00:02:05,900
And she plants it.

68
00:02:06,187 --> 00:02:07,786
In things that grow wool.

69
00:02:08,185 --> 00:02:09,996
That wool grows more wool.

70
00:02:10,333 --> 00:02:12,218
And that wool grows even more wool.

71
00:02:12,429 --> 00:02:14,527
Wool on wool on wool.

72
00:02:15,251 --> 00:02:16,686
It's called compounding.

73
00:02:17,685 --> 00:02:20,182
Remember the debt snowball from the credit card video?

74
00:02:21,030 --> 00:02:22,554
This is the same snowball.

75
00:02:23,278 --> 00:02:24,664
Rolling the other way.

76
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,148
Let's do the math.

77
00:02:26,872 --> 00:02:30,605
Say a sheep puts five hundred dollars a month into things that grow.

78
00:02:30,942 --> 00:02:33,489
And they grow about seven percent a year on average.

79
00:02:34,026 --> 00:02:36,821
Start at twenty-five, stop at sixty-five.

80
00:02:37,109 --> 00:02:39,231
About one point three million dollars.

81
00:02:39,693 --> 00:02:42,402
But the sheep only put in two hundred and forty thousand.

82
00:02:43,026 --> 00:02:43,962
The other million?

83
00:02:44,499 --> 00:02:45,611
Grew by itself.

84
00:02:46,234 --> 00:02:48,282
Now start at thirty-five instead.

85
00:02:48,818 --> 00:02:50,767
About six hundred and ten thousand.

86
00:02:51,390 --> 00:02:52,527
Ten years late.

87
00:02:53,150 --> 00:02:54,486
Less than half the wool.

88
00:02:54,674 --> 00:02:55,859
The money didn't change.

89
00:02:55,985 --> 00:02:57,407
Time did.

90
00:02:57,747 --> 00:02:59,420
And here's the craziest part.

91
00:02:59,819 --> 00:03:00,556
Two sheep.

92
00:03:00,769 --> 00:03:01,755
Molly and Gary.

93
00:03:01,917 --> 00:03:05,062
Molly invests three hundred a month from twenty-five to thirty-five.

94
00:03:05,687 --> 00:03:06,524
Then she stops.

95
00:03:06,573 --> 00:03:07,859
Never adds another cent.

96
00:03:08,484 --> 00:03:10,106
Gary starts at thirty-five.

97
00:03:10,568 --> 00:03:12,740
Three hundred a month, for thirty whole years.

98
00:03:12,990 --> 00:03:14,938
Gary puts in three times more wool.

99
00:03:15,225 --> 00:03:16,635
Who has more at sixty-five?

100
00:03:17,097 --> 00:03:17,646
Molly.

101
00:03:17,933 --> 00:03:22,540
About three hundred and ninety-five thousand, against Gary's three hundred and sixty-five.

102
00:03:22,877 --> 00:03:25,361
Ten years of planting beat thirty years of catching up.

103
00:03:25,823 --> 00:03:28,532
Because Molly's wool had more time to grow wool.

104
00:03:28,695 --> 00:03:30,555
Gary's still confused.

105
00:03:30,891 --> 00:03:32,951
Want proof from the real world?

106
00:03:34,311 --> 00:03:35,397
Warren Buffett.

107
00:03:36,022 --> 00:03:38,169
One of the richest investors in history.

108
00:03:38,568 --> 00:03:40,728
He bought his first stock at eleven years old.

109
00:03:41,352 --> 00:03:43,225
He's a brilliant investor, sure.

110
00:03:43,849 --> 00:03:45,409
But here's what people miss.

111
00:03:45,809 --> 00:03:49,917
The vast majority of his fortune came after his sixty-fifth birthday.

112
00:03:50,253 --> 00:03:52,238
His real secret isn't genius.

113
00:03:52,425 --> 00:03:53,324
It's time.

114
00:03:53,724 --> 00:03:56,358
He started early and never stopped.

115
00:03:56,691 --> 00:03:58,888
So who owns the things that grow wool?

116
00:03:59,737 --> 00:04:04,231
In America, the richest ten percent own about ninety percent of all stocks.

117
00:04:04,768 --> 00:04:07,751
So when companies grow, guess whose wool grows?

118
00:04:08,213 --> 00:04:08,888
Theirs.

119
00:04:09,511 --> 00:04:12,408
And when prices go up, guess who feels it?

120
00:04:12,945 --> 00:04:13,456
Gary.

121
00:04:13,532 --> 00:04:14,980
Remember my first video?

122
00:04:15,379 --> 00:04:18,163
Inflation melts wool sitting in a drawer.

123
00:04:18,788 --> 00:04:23,069
But things that grow wool usually grow faster than inflation over time.

124
00:04:23,468 --> 00:04:26,377
So the sheep with savings in a drawer gets poorer.

125
00:04:27,002 --> 00:04:29,049
And the sheep with trees gets richer.

126
00:04:29,673 --> 00:04:32,045
Without either of them doing anything.

127
00:04:32,386 --> 00:04:33,922
But that's not the worst part.

128
00:04:34,645 --> 00:04:37,504
Where do you think the interest on Gary's credit card goes?

129
00:04:38,228 --> 00:04:39,701
To whoever owns the bank.

130
00:04:40,101 --> 00:04:41,874
Gary pays twenty-two percent.

131
00:04:42,411 --> 00:04:43,697
Someone else collects it.

132
00:04:44,096 --> 00:04:45,119
Debt is a pipe.

133
00:04:45,656 --> 00:04:48,940
It carries wool from the sheep who owe to the sheep who own.

134
00:04:49,476 --> 00:04:50,200
Every month.

135
00:04:50,262 --> 00:04:51,661
Automatically.

136
00:04:51,993 --> 00:04:53,678
And debt isn't the only pipe.

137
00:04:54,303 --> 00:04:56,101
Every month, Gary pays rent.

138
00:04:56,725 --> 00:04:57,686
Where does it go?

139
00:04:58,222 --> 00:04:59,971
To the sheep who owns the house.

140
00:05:00,594 --> 00:05:02,530
Gary's wool pays off her house.

141
00:05:03,066 --> 00:05:07,535
And in thirty years, she owns a house, and Gary owns thirty years of receipts.

142
00:05:07,722 --> 00:05:09,171
Renting isn't always bad.

143
00:05:09,633 --> 00:05:11,156
Sometimes it's the smart move.

144
00:05:11,692 --> 00:05:14,264
But only if the money you save goes into trees.

145
00:05:14,888 --> 00:05:17,060
Not into a bigger car.

146
00:05:17,402 --> 00:05:18,364
And the rules?

147
00:05:18,526 --> 00:05:20,461
Written by people who read the rules.

148
00:05:20,861 --> 00:05:25,180
In the U.S., wool from a job can be taxed up to thirty-seven percent.

149
00:05:25,580 --> 00:05:28,039
Wool from investments held over a year?

150
00:05:28,887 --> 00:05:31,335
Usually zero, fifteen, or twenty percent.

151
00:05:31,958 --> 00:05:32,782
Same wool.

152
00:05:32,970 --> 00:05:33,943
Different tax.

153
00:05:34,568 --> 00:05:38,563
And when rich sheep need cash, they often don't sell their trees.

154
00:05:38,750 --> 00:05:39,911
They borrow against them.

155
00:05:40,161 --> 00:05:41,409
The trees keep growing.

156
00:05:41,871 --> 00:05:42,633
No sale.

157
00:05:42,795 --> 00:05:44,043
No tax on a sale.

158
00:05:44,442 --> 00:05:45,304
It's legal.

159
00:05:45,928 --> 00:05:48,662
It's just in the part of the rulebook nobody reads.

160
00:05:48,999 --> 00:05:49,611
I read it.

161
00:05:49,944 --> 00:05:51,992
So what's Gary's plan to get rich?

162
00:05:52,616 --> 00:05:53,489
The lottery.

163
00:05:53,652 --> 00:05:58,134
The odds of winning the Powerball jackpot are one in two hundred and ninety-two million.

164
00:05:58,471 --> 00:06:00,817
You're far more likely to be struck by lightning.

165
00:06:01,280 --> 00:06:04,226
And you've probably heard that most lottery winners go broke.

166
00:06:04,275 --> 00:06:05,350
That famous number?

167
00:06:05,886 --> 00:06:07,534
Nobody can find where it came from.

168
00:06:08,158 --> 00:06:09,968
But the lesson behind it is real.

169
00:06:10,181 --> 00:06:12,327
A pile of wool is not the same as a tree.

170
00:06:12,665 --> 00:06:13,800
A pile gets eaten.

171
00:06:14,138 --> 00:06:16,410
A tree feeds you forever.

172
00:06:16,745 --> 00:06:18,318
Now, I promised you a story.

173
00:06:18,530 --> 00:06:20,103
Brattleboro, Vermont.

174
00:06:20,266 --> 00:06:21,939
A man named Ronald Read.

175
00:06:22,151 --> 00:06:24,723
He pumped gas for about twenty-five years.

176
00:06:25,122 --> 00:06:26,745
Later, he worked as a janitor.

177
00:06:27,082 --> 00:06:28,829
He drove an old used car.

178
00:06:29,292 --> 00:06:30,676
He cut his own firewood.

179
00:06:31,526 --> 00:06:32,799
Nobody thought he had money.

180
00:06:33,011 --> 00:06:35,321
He died in twenty fourteen, at ninety-two.

181
00:06:35,783 --> 00:06:38,841
And his town found out he was worth about eight million dollars.

182
00:06:39,690 --> 00:06:40,427
Eight million.

183
00:06:40,614 --> 00:06:41,175
How?

184
00:06:41,800 --> 00:06:44,346
He bought shares in big, boring companies.

185
00:06:44,746 --> 00:06:45,719
And then he held them.

186
00:06:46,057 --> 00:06:47,018
For decades.

187
00:06:47,118 --> 00:06:48,029
No genius.

188
00:06:48,154 --> 00:06:48,966
No lottery.

189
00:06:49,053 --> 00:06:49,814
Just time.

190
00:06:50,352 --> 00:06:53,535
He left millions to his local library and his hospital.

191
00:06:54,159 --> 00:06:55,707
A janitor beat Wall Street.

192
00:06:55,744 --> 00:07:03,809
And around the same time, plenty of Wall Street executives with huge salaries borrowed big, spent big, and lost almost everything.

193
00:07:04,146 --> 00:07:06,094
High income doesn't make you rich.

194
00:07:06,493 --> 00:07:07,629
Owning does.

195
00:07:07,968 --> 00:07:11,188
But wolf, I don't have any wool left over to plant.

196
00:07:11,913 --> 00:07:12,449
Fair.

197
00:07:12,574 --> 00:07:13,785
Then start in this order.

198
00:07:14,510 --> 00:07:14,996
One.

199
00:07:15,058 --> 00:07:16,008
A small cushion.

200
00:07:16,032 --> 00:07:19,054
A few hundred, then one month of bills, in a separate account.

201
00:07:19,677 --> 00:07:22,387
So one broken tooth doesn't go on a credit card.

202
00:07:23,011 --> 00:07:23,511
Two.

203
00:07:23,635 --> 00:07:25,084
Kill the high-interest debt.

204
00:07:25,158 --> 00:07:29,277
Paying off a card at twenty-two percent is like a guaranteed twenty-two percent return.

205
00:07:29,377 --> 00:07:31,549
No investment I know promises you that.

206
00:07:32,399 --> 00:07:32,935
Three.

207
00:07:33,123 --> 00:07:34,309
Then plant trees.

208
00:07:34,397 --> 00:07:35,183
Every month.

209
00:07:35,808 --> 00:07:36,869
Small is fine.

210
00:07:37,592 --> 00:07:40,626
Every rich tree was a tiny seed once.

211
00:07:40,961 --> 00:07:43,021
So how does a sheep start growing trees?

212
00:07:43,358 --> 00:07:44,157
Rule one.

213
00:07:44,882 --> 00:07:46,104
Pay yourself first.

214
00:07:46,266 --> 00:07:50,536
The day your wool arrives, move a piece of it somewhere you can't easily spend it.

215
00:07:50,873 --> 00:07:51,772
Automatically.

216
00:07:52,109 --> 00:07:53,945
Before Gary-brain wakes up.

217
00:07:54,482 --> 00:07:55,380
Rule two.

218
00:07:55,630 --> 00:07:57,054
Own things that grow.

219
00:07:57,216 --> 00:08:00,923
For most sheep, the boring answer is a low-cost index fund.

220
00:08:01,211 --> 00:08:03,719
A tiny piece of hundreds of companies at once.

221
00:08:04,119 --> 00:08:04,706
Boring.

222
00:08:04,769 --> 00:08:05,243
Cheap.

223
00:08:05,268 --> 00:08:05,979
Works.

224
00:08:06,166 --> 00:08:07,103
Rule three.

225
00:08:07,228 --> 00:08:08,363
Watch the fees.

226
00:08:08,826 --> 00:08:11,360
A one percent yearly fee sounds tiny.

227
00:08:11,485 --> 00:08:14,905
Over forty years, it can eat about a quarter of your wool.

228
00:08:15,530 --> 00:08:16,403
Rule four.

229
00:08:16,441 --> 00:08:18,576
Don't feed your raises to wool eaters.

230
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:20,248
Rule five.

231
00:08:20,711 --> 00:08:21,685
Start now.

232
00:08:21,821 --> 00:08:22,907
Even small.

233
00:08:23,307 --> 00:08:26,928
Twenty dollars planted today beats a thousand planted never.

234
00:08:27,777 --> 00:08:32,421
And when the market falls, and it will, don't pull your trees out of the ground.

235
00:08:33,045 --> 00:08:34,556
Not financial advice.

236
00:08:35,092 --> 00:08:36,690
I'm a wolf.

237
00:08:37,035 --> 00:08:39,332
And you're wondering, why would a wolf tell you this?

238
00:08:39,732 --> 00:08:40,244
Simple.

239
00:08:40,493 --> 00:08:42,814
A sheep with trees doesn't need my credit card.

240
00:08:43,214 --> 00:08:44,501
Doesn't need my loans.

241
00:08:45,124 --> 00:08:46,485
That's bad for business.

242
00:08:46,610 --> 00:08:48,021
But you watched this far.

243
00:08:48,745 --> 00:08:50,767
So I guess you earned it.

244
00:08:51,108 --> 00:08:51,994
Cheat sheet.

245
00:08:52,131 --> 00:08:53,492
Working grows wool.

246
00:08:53,780 --> 00:08:55,963
Owning grows wool while you sleep.

247
00:08:56,301 --> 00:08:58,324
Don't feed your raises to wool eaters.

248
00:08:58,660 --> 00:09:00,084
Wool grows on wool.

249
00:09:00,220 --> 00:09:01,369
Time beats amount.

250
00:09:01,431 --> 00:09:02,518
Watch the fees.

251
00:09:02,643 --> 00:09:04,141
Plant your first tree this week.

252
00:09:04,428 --> 00:09:06,688
Send this to the Gary in your life.

253
00:09:07,027 --> 00:09:07,975
And Gary?

254
00:09:08,138 --> 00:09:09,936
Gary finally planted a tree.

255
00:09:10,660 --> 00:09:11,809
He's very proud.

256
00:09:12,096 --> 00:09:13,981
He planted his last piece of wool.

257
00:09:14,605 --> 00:09:15,554
Hello, sheep.

258
00:09:15,953 --> 00:09:19,723
If you don't want to end up bald like Gary… subscribe.

259
00:09:20,448 --> 00:09:23,107
Gary's retirement plan is a lottery ticket.
